\begin{frame}{How to read the stage table}{every query ends in exactly one of three outcomes -- and which stages were skipped}
\small
\begin{itemize}\setlength\itemsep{4pt}
\item \highlight{$|D|<k$}: retrieval returned fewer than $k$ tables, so Stage 1 and Stage 2 are both skipped (infeasible).
\item \highlight{LP decisive}: the Stage 2 LP pre-check proves that no $k$ tables reach $F\ge0.3$, so the MILP is skipped (infeasible).
\item \highlight{MILP ran}: the LP was feasible and the MILP solved it (feasible). The MILP never failed after a feasible LP.
\item \highlight{S1 bypassed} ($P{=}D$): when $\alpha k\ge|D|$ the pool is all of $D$, so Stage 1 is not needed; Stage 2 still runs. \emph{S1 ran}: it picked $\alpha k$ tables out of $D$.
\end{itemize}
\vspace{2pt}
\textbf{Example} -- \texttt{tusSmall3}, $\alpha{=}10$, $k{=}20$: $92 = 2$ ($|D|<k$) $+\,27$ (LP infeasible) $+\,63$ (feasible). Of the 90 that reach Stage 1, it was bypassed for 76 and ran for 14.

\vspace{4pt}
\textbf{The optimizer cannot fix these failures:} Stage 1 and Stage 2 only choose among the tables in $D$, and $\alpha$ only widens the pool \emph{inside} $D$. If $D$ lacks tables with enough of the protected value, no selection can reach $\tau$.
\end{frame}

