\begin{frame}{How to read the precision/recall table}{the same two measures at each step of the pipeline}
\small
\begin{itemize}\setlength\itemsep{4pt}
\item $D$: tables retrieved (before any selection). $P$: the pool of $\alpha k$ tables chosen by Stage 1. $R$: the final $k$ tables.
\item \highlight{Precision stays high at every step}: almost everything retrieved is truly unionable, and selecting for fairness does not add wrong tables.
\item \highlight{Recall falls from $D$ to $R$ only because the sets shrink}: $D$ holds about 100 tables, $P$ holds $\alpha k$, $R$ holds $k$, while the ground truth is much larger. Precision and recall at $D$ do not depend on $k$ or $\alpha$.
\end{itemize}
